feat(webview): rewrite the settings UI in Vue 3

Completes the webview migration: the settings panel was the last vanilla DOM
surface, with ~100 kB of tab renderers spread over eight files.

Protocol
- `src/protocol/settings.ts` now owns the settings postMessage contract, the
  second half of the webview boundary alongside `messages.ts`. It types both
  directions (43 webview -> host messages, 6 host -> webview) and the `tabData`
  payload shape per tab, and exposes an `isSettingsTabId` runtime guard.
- The previous declarations lived only in the old webview's switch statements
  and had drifted from `settings-panel.ts`; this file was derived by walking
  every `case` in that handler.

Webview
- `stores/settings.ts` holds the tab list, per-tab data cache, dirty/saving
  state and the optimistic patch path; `composables/useSettingsLink.ts` is the
  inbound router, mirroring `useHostLink.ts` on the chat side.
- `pages/SettingsPage.vue` rebuilds the nav shell from the legacy markup.
- The eight tabs are components under `components/settings/`: Models (split
  further into providers, provider detail, compat, headers, OAuth and API-key
  panes), Agents, Prompts, Skills, MCP, Commit, System Prompt and General —
  plus shared editors (`SettingFieldEditor`, `SecretInput`, `ScopeSelect`,
  `ItemRow`, `ConfirmBar`, `SectionHeader`).
- `styles/settings.css` is reused unchanged; the components emit the class
  names it already targets.

Known host-side gaps found while porting (left untouched, for a follow-up):
- `refresh` shares a `case` with `tabLoad`, so a whole-panel reload is not
  possible.
- Saving settings / system prompt / commit config only answers `saved`, never a
  fresh `tabData`, so the webview has to commit optimistically.
- `saved{what:"mcp"}` and `writeModelsJson` have host handlers but no UI entry.

Verified: pnpm run build (clean), pnpm run test (lint + typecheck + vitest).